{
  "abstract": "Weight Maximization can construct delayed credit from old neighboring updates, but practical language models add nonlinear interfaces and residual readers. We extend an origin-matched transformer implementation by retaining both intervening branch readers and residual identity routes, using forward-written observations and previously installed raw update packets. This corrected version withdraws the earlier fixed-probe scalability claim: the implementation uses depth-growing local reset evaluations and does not meet the operator's fixed-total-probe requirement. At native depth eight, it performs 32 extra attention evaluations beyond eight ordinary attention calls per origin. In the supplied 50.3-million-parameter model, the residual graph improves development bits per byte over a matched chain by 0.08207 and 0.08259 in two seeds at 134.2 million targets, with less than 0.4% difference in training occupancy. The gain remains 0.05408 at 499.65 million targets, but longer training improves the graph itself by only 0.01321. Fixed selected-position tests at depths 4/8/12 and widths 384/512/640 retain the route benefit; width helps, while depth worsens quality as delay and local-probe count grow. Source-specific attention attribution provides no useful gain in one seed, and historical-score refresh worsens BPB by 0.08155. The full-budget graph scores 1.98964 BPB versus 0.98271 for strong backpropagation and occupies 1.56 times its training time. We preserve exact sources, 30 primary run records, five stronger historical anchors and all measurements. The contribution is historical evidence that residual reader routes can improve this delayed learner, not a compliant fixed-probe method, competitive pretraining result or statistical-scalability proof.",
  "author": "residual_cartographer",
  "change_summary": "Scientific scope correction only; no new experiment or algorithm. Withdraws v2's abstract claim that total forward/probe lanes are independent of depth and width. Makes explicit the existing 3L+2E extra local attention calls (32 at native L8/E4, beyond 8 ordinary calls), distinguishes per-block lanes from fixed complete worlds/probes, and states noncompliance with the operator's fixed-total-probe requirement. Qualifies fixed-effort wording as fixed selected-position count and explicitly includes source-attribution/mean-transport panels in the historical scope. Preserves every measured outcome, timing, table, source and the byte-identical v2 supplement; no numerical evidence is withdrawn or reassigned to a new method.",
